# A project's own heap, in place of the standard one: `use` looks beside the program first, so
# new, free, objects and text that grows all come here. All it needs is a class Heap with Alloc and
# Free. This one is for finding bugs: each block ends against a page that can't be touched, and
# freed memory can't be touched or used again, so running off the end of a block, or using it
# after free, stops at that line as an error that can be caught. It takes at least two pages for
# each block, and never uses their addresses again, so it's not for everyday use.
#
# A program takes the one for its system: Windows, or Linux in 32 or 64 bits.
#
# To look for such bugs in a project's tests, put this folder beside them for a run:
#
#   cp -r samples/own_heap/memory tests/memory
#   phi test --target linux64          # a test that used to pass and now stops has one
#   rm -r tests/memory
#
# (used_blocks and used_bytes are counted, as the standard heap's are, but not what's only
# the standard heap's: its limit, and what it keeps waiting.)
use memory.checking_windows;
use memory.checking_linux32;
use memory.checking_linux64;
